2026-11-24 19:01 Cause 1 (numbers) reproduced on a long-double perl (x86_64-linux-ld 5.40.1 under ~/.cache on reuben): t/10, t/14, t/15, t/24 red. js_number compares sprintf('%.*e') read back as NV with the double; on a wider NV the read-back is a long double, so no shorter digit count matches and 17 digits come out; 1e999 is finite there. t/27 is a different cause: 3 of 4 reports are -DDEBUGGING perls, reproduced with Debian's debugperl in a container: sources_read on 300 KB takes 41.8 s (plain perl 0.47 s), so the parse is superlinear under DEBUGGING; armv6l is slow hardware (suite 48x slower).

2026-11-24 19:09 t/27 cause: perl's intrpvar.h sets PL_utf8cache to -1 under DEBUGGING (or PERL_UTF8_CACHE_ASSERT), so every UTF-8 char/byte offset lookup is verified by a linear scan from the start; any walk by offset over a flagged text is quadratic there by design (debugperl: 30 records 0.96 s, 60 records 3.79 s; the same text downgraded to bytes 0.10/0.15 s). Not a lib bug; armv6l is slow but linear. Plan: t/27 measures with ${^UTF8CACHE} = 1 and against the same text as bytes on the same machine instead of an absolute 3 s; own commit.

